0,Music of Madagascar - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Music_of_Madagascar,Distribution of Malagasy musical forms Malagasy music is highly melodic and distinguishes itself from many traditions of mainland Africa by the predominance of chordophone relative to percussion instruments. [4] Musical instruments and vocal styles found in Madagascar represent a blend of widespread commonalities and highly localized traditions. A common vocal style among the Merina and ...,"Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
1,Merina people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merina_people,"The Merina people, also known as Imerina, Antimerina, Borizany or Ambaniandro[3] and formerly called Amboalambo, are the largest ethnic group in Madagascar. [4][5] They are the ""highlander"" Malagasy ethnic group of the African island and one of the country's eighteen official ethnic groups. [6][7] Their origins are diverse, primarily from the mixing of early Austronesian settlers who arrived ...","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
2,Traditional music in Madagascar,https://www.musicinafrica.net/magazine/traditional-music-madagascar/,"Traditional music in Madagascar formed its identity with influences from various horizons: the Austronesian peoples on the one hand, and African, Arab and European migratory populations on the other. Such variety made it impossible to specify, in time and space, the true origin of traditional music in Madagascar. What is certain is that traditional music was probably heard for the first time ...","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
3,Valiha and Malagasy Music: Traditional Instruments and Live Performance ...,https://voyagiste-madagascar.com/valiha-and-malagasy-music-traditional-instruments-and-live-performance-spots/,"The valiha's origins trace to Southeast Asia — specifically to similar instruments found in the Philippines, Indonesia and Vietnam — which aligns with the well-documented Austronesian origin of the Malagasy people, who migrated from the Indonesian archipelago roughly 1,500 years ago. Over centuries, the instrument developed a distinctly Malagasy character: the tuning, the playing ...","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
4,Music of Madagascar — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Music_of_Madagascar,"The music of Madagascar comprises the diverse traditional and contemporary styles performed by the Malagasy people, reflecting the island's settlement by Austronesian migrants from Southeast Asia around the 5th to 10th centuries CE and subsequent Bantu arrivals from East Africa, which produced a distinctive synthesis of melodic chordophones and rhythmic percussion. [1][2] Traditional Malagasy ...","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
5,Madagascar Malagasy People: an African Mixed Race (Black & Asian ...,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YMOHPsDDE5g,"Unity in Diversity: The Malagasy Identity In music, art, and dance, the Malagasy people express their unique identity, drawing on both African and Austronesian traditions.","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
6,Music of Madagascar explained,https://everything.explained.today/Music_of_Madagascar/,"Music of Madagascar explained The highly diverse and distinctive music of Madagascar has been shaped by the musical traditions of Southeast Asia, Africa, Oceania, Arabia, Portugal, England, France and the United States over time as indigenous people, immigrants, and colonists have made the island their home. [1] Traditional instruments reflect these widespread origins: the Malagasy ...","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
7,Austronesian Music - Melodigging,https://www.melodigging.com/genre/austronesian-music,"Austronesian music is an umbrella term for the diverse musical traditions of Austronesian-speaking peoples, whose homelands stretch from Taiwan through Island Southeast Asia to Oceania and Madagascar.","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
8,The Merina people - Vivy Travel Madagascar,https://www.vivytravel.com/the-merina-people/,"The Malagasy language, with its Merina dialect, is an Austronesian language that has incorporated Swahili, Arabic, and European influences over the centuries. The Merina celebrate numerous festivals and rituals, including the famadihana, or turning of the ancestors, and the famorana, or circumcision.","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
9,"Madagascar People: History, Culture and Traditions",https://onlybyland.com/madagascar-people/,"The Malagasy and French languages signify Madagascar's complex history and colonial inheritance. Madagascar traditions, from music to cuisine, reflect a harmonious blend of local and international influences. Environmental and economic challenges present opportunities to prioritize sustainable tourism and cultural preservation.","Malagasy, Merina",Austronesian
